  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh confidence in a prolonged stretch of dry weather across\neastern NY and western New England through this week and even\ninto this weekend as high pressure dominates the weather\npattern. With our ~1030-1035hPa high centered in western Quebec\ntoday and a slowly strengthening nor'easter well off the North\nCarolina/mid-Atlantic coast, the pressure gradient overhead will\ntighten enough today to promote breezy northerly winds. The\nstrongest winds will likely be in the north-south oriented\nHudson Valley as winds funnel down the valley with a 50 to 75%\nchance for wind gusts to exceed 20mph until 21-23 UTC when the \natmosphere decouples. Otherwise, we will enjoy seasonable\ntemperatures this afternoon under abundant sunshine. The \npersistent northerly winds will also maintain dry air advection\nwith PWATs likely dropping towards 0.25\" this afternoon. High\npressure builds further east tomorrow, reducing the pressure \ngradient and leading to weaker winds with some cirrus clouds\nfrom our nor'easter spreading overhead. By Thursday, a moisture\nstarved shortwave trough tracks through southern Canada, \nhelping direct our nor'easter further north and east before\nit exits out to sea. While there is some uncertainty within the\nlatest ensemble guidance on far west the sfc low tracks, there \nis a high confidence that it remains well offshore to not pose \nany direct impacts to our area. The trough axis will be too dry \nto support precip but winds should become a bit breezy ahead of \nand behind the boundary as cold/dry air advection ensues. In\nfact, there is a 30 to 50% chance for wind gusts to exceed \n30mph in the 24hr period from 2AM Thurs to 2AM Fri. Canadian \nhigh pressure builds overhead Friday into the weekend, \ncontinuing our sunny and dry stretch with seasonably cool \ntemperatures Friday. Favorable radiational cooling conditions \nFriday and Saturday night will likely support chilly overnight \ntemperatures with greater than a 75% chance for Fri night lows \nto fall below 35 degrees.",
